content.text:
There is **no database**. Records live in a JavaScript `Map` in the Node process’s RAM. Restart the server and everything is gone.

**Path:** HTTP route → `DnsService.addRecord` → `DnsStore.add` → `Map` keyed by hostname.

When the app starts, `buildApp` creates one store and hands it to the service:

```25:28:backend/src/app.js
  const dnsStore = store ?? new DnsStore();
  const jobQueue = queue ?? new JobQueue({ maxSize: QUEUE_MAX_SIZE });
  const queryLogStore = queryLogs ?? new QueryLogStore({ maxEntries: QUERY_LOG_MAX_ENTRIES });
  const dnsService = new DnsService(dnsStore, { queue: jobQueue });
```

`POST /api/dns` does not write files or talk to Postgres/Redis. It calls that in-memory service and returns 201:

```11:14:backend/src/routes/dns.js
  fastify.post("/api/dns", { schema: addRecordSchema }, async (request, reply) => {
    const { type, hostname, value, ttl = null } = request.body;
    const record = dnsService.addRecord(type, hostname, value, ttl ?? null);
    return reply.code(201).send(record.toResponse());
```

`addRecord` then:

1. Normalizes hostname (`Example.COM.` → `example.com`) and validates the value (IPv4 for A, hostname for CNAME).
2. Checks DNS rules against whatever is already in the Map (no duplicate tuple, CNAME cannot sit next to A records, no CNAME cycle).
3. Builds a `DnsRecord` object in memory (`hostname`, `type`, `value`, `createdAt`, optional `ttl` / `expiresAt`).
4. Calls `this.store.add(record)`.
5. If `ttl` was set, enqueues a delayed expire job (also in memory).

```36:46:backend/src/service.js
    const now = new Date();
    const expiresAt = ttl != null ? new Date(now.getTime() + ttl * 1000) : null;
    const record = new DnsRecord({
      hostname,
      type: recordType,
      value,
      createdAt: now,
      ttl,
      expiresAt,
    });
    this.store.add(record);
```

The “database” is this Map:

```1:27:backend/src/store.js
export class DnsStore {
  constructor() {
    this._records = new Map();
  }
  // ...
  add(record) {
    const existing = this._records.get(record.hostname);
    if (existing) {
      existing.push(record);
    } else {
      this._records.set(record.hostname, [record]);
    }
    return record;
  }
```

After two A records and a CNAME, RAM looks like:

```
Map {
  "example.com"       => [ DnsRecord(A, 192.168.1.1), DnsRecord(A, 192.168.1.2) ]
  "alias.example.com" => [ DnsRecord(CNAME, example.com) ]
}
```

Resolve/list/delete all read or splice that same Map. That is why Postman data disappears after `npm start` again, and why two API processes would not share records.
